<section class="learn">
  <h2 class="learn-title">How to play craps</h2>
  <p class="learn-intro">
    Craps looks chaotic — a crowded layout, a dozen bets, people shouting. It isn't.
    The whole game runs on one simple loop, and once you see the loop the table stops
    being intimidating. Start at the top and work down; everything here can be tried on
    the table above.
  </p>

  <details class="accordion">
    <summary>1 &middot; What craps actually is</summary>
    <div class="accordion-body">
      <p>
        One player — the <b>shooter</b> — rolls two dice. Everyone at the table bets on
        what those dice will total. That's it. You are never playing against a dealer and
        never playing against the other players; you're all betting on the same dice, and
        most of the table is usually betting the same direction.
      </p>
      <p>
        Because two dice can only total 2 through 12, and because some totals are far more
        likely than others, every bet on the layout has a fixed, published payout. Nothing
        is hidden. Learning craps is mostly learning which of those bets are priced fairly
        and which are not.
      </p>
      <p class="learn-note">
        The table above is a practice table — the chips aren't real and there's no money
        involved. It's for learning the loop and testing a betting plan.
      </p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details class="accordion">
    <summary>2 &middot; The loop: come-out roll, then the point</summary>
    <div class="accordion-body">
      <p>
        Every round has two phases. The first roll of a round is the <b>come-out roll</b>,
        and the status bar on the table above shows <b>OFF (come-out)</b> when you're in it.
      </p>
      <p>On the come-out roll, a <b>Pass Line</b> bet resolves immediately if it can:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><b>7 or 11</b> — Pass Line wins right away, even money.</li>
        <li><b>2, 3, or 12</b> — called <i>craps</i>. Pass Line loses right away.</li>
        <li><b>4, 5, 6, 8, 9, or 10</b> — nothing wins or loses. That number becomes
          <b>the point</b>, and the round moves into its second phase.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Once a point is set, the goal flips. Now the shooter is trying to roll
        <b>that number again before rolling a 7</b>. Roll the point first and the Pass Line
        wins. Roll a 7 first — a <b>seven out</b> — and the Pass Line loses and the round ends.
        Any other total in between simply doesn't matter to the Pass Line; the shooter keeps
        rolling.
      </p>

      <figure class="learn-figure">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 300 452" role="img" aria-labelledby="flowTitle flowDesc" class="dgm">
          <title id="flowTitle">How a round of craps flows</title>
          <desc id="flowDesc">The come-out roll either wins on 7 or 11, loses on 2, 3 or 12,
            or sets a point of 4, 5, 6, 8, 9 or 10. Once a point is set, rolling the point
            again wins and rolling a 7 loses.</desc>

          <!-- Phase 1 -->
          <text x="150" y="14" class="dgm-phase" text-anchor="middle">PHASE 1</text>
          <rect x="70" y="22" width="160" height="34" rx="6" class="dgm-box-gold"/>
          <text x="150" y="44" class="dgm-lede" text-anchor="middle">COME-OUT ROLL</text>

          <!-- stem + branch rail -->
          <path d="M150 56 V 74" class="dgm-line"/>
          <path d="M48 74 H 252" class="dgm-line"/>
          <path d="M48 74 V 88 M150 74 V 88 M252 74 V 88" class="dgm-line"/>

          <!-- three outcomes -->
          <rect x="6" y="88" width="84" height="52" rx="6" class="dgm-box-win"/>
          <text x="48" y="108" class="dgm-num" text-anchor="middle">7 or 11</text>
          <text x="48" y="127" class="dgm-win" text-anchor="middle">WIN</text>

          <rect x="108" y="88" width="84" height="52" rx="6" class="dgm-box-lose"/>
          <text x="150" y="108" class="dgm-num" text-anchor="middle">2 3 12</text>
          <text x="150" y="127" class="dgm-lose" text-anchor="middle">LOSE</text>

          <rect x="210" y="88" width="84" height="52" rx="6" class="dgm-box"/>
          <text x="252" y="105" class="dgm-num-sm" text-anchor="middle">4 5 6</text>
          <text x="252" y="121" class="dgm-num-sm" text-anchor="middle">8 9 10</text>
          <text x="252" y="135" class="dgm-tag" text-anchor="middle">POINT SET</text>

          <!-- down to phase 2 -->
          <path d="M252 140 V 168 H 150 V 190" class="dgm-line"/>
          <path d="M146 184 L150 191 L154 184 Z" class="dgm-arrow"/>

          <text x="150" y="216" class="dgm-phase" text-anchor="middle">PHASE 2</text>
          <rect x="52" y="224" width="196" height="46" rx="6" class="dgm-box-gold"/>
          <text x="150" y="243" class="dgm-lede" text-anchor="middle">POINT IS ON</text>
          <text x="150" y="260" class="dgm-sub" text-anchor="middle">keep rolling</text>

          <path d="M150 270 V 288" class="dgm-line"/>
          <path d="M74 288 H 226" class="dgm-line"/>
          <path d="M74 288 V 302 M226 288 V 302" class="dgm-line"/>

          <rect x="18" y="302" width="112" height="66" rx="6" class="dgm-box-win"/>
          <text x="74" y="322" class="dgm-num-sm" text-anchor="middle">point rolls</text>
          <text x="74" y="338" class="dgm-num-sm" text-anchor="middle">again</text>
          <text x="74" y="357" class="dgm-win" text-anchor="middle">WIN</text>

          <rect x="170" y="302" width="112" height="66" rx="6" class="dgm-box-lose"/>
          <text x="226" y="322" class="dgm-num-sm" text-anchor="middle">7 rolls</text>
          <text x="226" y="338" class="dgm-num-sm" text-anchor="middle">first</text>
          <text x="226" y="357" class="dgm-lose" text-anchor="middle">SEVEN OUT</text>

          <!-- loop back -->
          <path d="M74 368 V 396 H 150 V 414" class="dgm-line-dash"/>
          <path d="M226 368 V 396 H 150" class="dgm-line-dash"/>
          <path d="M146 408 L150 415 L154 408 Z" class="dgm-arrow"/>
          <text x="150" y="436" class="dgm-tag" text-anchor="middle">NEW COME-OUT ROLL</text>
        </svg>
        <figcaption>
          Anything that isn't the point or a 7 during Phase 2 leaves the Pass Line alone —
          the shooter just rolls again.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details class="accordion">
    <summary>3 &middot; Why 7 decides everything</summary>
    <div class="accordion-body">
      <p>
        Two dice have 36 possible combinations, and they are not spread evenly. There are
        six different ways to roll a 7 (<span class="pips">&#9856;&#9861;</span>,
        <span class="pips">&#9857;&#9860;</span>, <span class="pips">&#9858;&#9859;</span>,
        and each of those reversed) but only one way to roll a 2 and one way to roll a 12.
      </p>

      <figure class="learn-figure">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 300 316" role="img" aria-labelledby="freqTitle freqDesc" class="dgm">
          <title id="freqTitle">Ways to roll each total with two dice</title>
          <desc id="freqDesc">Out of 36 combinations: 7 has six ways; 6 and 8 have five each;
            5 and 9 have four each; 4 and 10 have three each; 3 and 11 have two each;
            2 and 12 have one each.</desc>

          <text x="0" y="10" class="dgm-axis">TOTAL</text>
          <text x="44" y="10" class="dgm-axis">WAYS TO ROLL IT (out of 36)</text>

          <g class="freq-rows">
            <!-- total, count -->
            <!-- Bar width is strictly proportional: 34 user units per way,
                 anchored at x=40. 1 way = 34, 6 ways = 204. -->
            <g><text x="28" y="34" class="dgm-rowlab" text-anchor="end">2</text><path d="M40 22 H 70 a4 4 0 0 1 4 4 V 34 a4 4 0 0 1 -4 4 H 40 Z" class="bar"/><text x="82" y="34" class="dgm-val">1</text></g>
            <g><text x="28" y="58" class="dgm-rowlab" text-anchor="end">3</text><path d="M40 46 H 104 a4 4 0 0 1 4 4 V 58 a4 4 0 0 1 -4 4 H 40 Z" class="bar"/><text x="116" y="58" class="dgm-val">2</text></g>
            <g><text x="28" y="82" class="dgm-rowlab" text-anchor="end">4</text><path d="M40 70 H 138 a4 4 0 0 1 4 4 V 82 a4 4 0 0 1 -4 4 H 40 Z" class="bar"/><text x="150" y="82" class="dgm-val">3</text></g>
            <g><text x="28" y="106" class="dgm-rowlab" text-anchor="end">5</text><path d="M40 94 H 172 a4 4 0 0 1 4 4 V 106 a4 4 0 0 1 -4 4 H 40 Z" class="bar"/><text x="184" y="106" class="dgm-val">4</text></g>
            <g><text x="28" y="130" class="dgm-rowlab" text-anchor="end">6</text><path d="M40 118 H 206 a4 4 0 0 1 4 4 V 130 a4 4 0 0 1 -4 4 H 40 Z" class="bar"/><text x="218" y="130" class="dgm-val">5</text></g>
            <g><text x="28" y="154" class="dgm-rowlab-hi" text-anchor="end">7</text><path d="M40 142 H 240 a4 4 0 0 1 4 4 V 154 a4 4 0 0 1 -4 4 H 40 Z" class="bar-hi"/><text x="252" y="154" class="dgm-val-hi">6</text></g>
            <g><text x="28" y="178" class="dgm-rowlab" text-anchor="end">8</text><path d="M40 166 H 206 a4 4 0 0 1 4 4 V 178 a4 4 0 0 1 -4 4 H 40 Z" class="bar"/><text x="218" y="178" class="dgm-val">5</text></g>
            <g><text x="28" y="202" class="dgm-rowlab" text-anchor="end">9</text><path d="M40 190 H 172 a4 4 0 0 1 4 4 V 202 a4 4 0 0 1 -4 4 H 40 Z" class="bar"/><text x="184" y="202" class="dgm-val">4</text></g>
            <g><text x="28" y="226" class="dgm-rowlab" text-anchor="end">10</text><path d="M40 214 H 138 a4 4 0 0 1 4 4 V 226 a4 4 0 0 1 -4 4 H 40 Z" class="bar"/><text x="150" y="226" class="dgm-val">3</text></g>
            <g><text x="28" y="250" class="dgm-rowlab" text-anchor="end">11</text><path d="M40 238 H 104 a4 4 0 0 1 4 4 V 250 a4 4 0 0 1 -4 4 H 40 Z" class="bar"/><text x="116" y="250" class="dgm-val">2</text></g>
            <g><text x="28" y="274" class="dgm-rowlab" text-anchor="end">12</text><path d="M40 262 H 70 a4 4 0 0 1 4 4 V 274 a4 4 0 0 1 -4 4 H 40 Z" class="bar"/><text x="82" y="274" class="dgm-val">1</text></g>
          </g>

          <path d="M40 288 H 260" class="dgm-rule"/>
          <text x="40" y="306" class="dgm-foot">7 is the most common roll — and the one that ends the round.</text>
        </svg>
        <figcaption>
          Every payout on the table is priced off this distribution.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p>
        This single fact drives the whole game. A 7 is the most likely total, which is why
        it wins instantly on the come-out — and why, once a point is set, it becomes the
        thing you're racing against. It also explains the payouts: a 6 or 8 is much easier
        to hit than a 4 or 10, so it pays less when it does.
      </p>
      <p>
        It also explains why <b>Any Seven</b> is the worst bet on the layout. Yes, 7 is the
        most common roll — but it pays only {{ odds.anySevenPayStr }} when the true odds are
        5:1, and that gap is where the house edge lives.
      </p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details class="accordion">
    <summary>4 &middot; Reading the table</summary>
    <div class="accordion-body">
      <p>
        A real craps layout is symmetrical — the same bets are printed on both ends so two
        crowds can play at once. Learn one half and you've learned the whole table. Here's
        that half, and where each area sits:
      </p>

      <figure class="learn-figure">
        <div class="figure-scroll">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 540 268" role="img" aria-labelledby="mapTitle mapDesc" class="dgm dgm-wide">
            <title id="mapTitle">Map of a craps table layout</title>
            <desc id="mapDesc">The Place number boxes run along the top, with Come below them,
              then the Field, then Don't Pass and the Pass Line along the outer edge.
              Hardways and one-roll proposition bets sit in a separate centre box.</desc>

            <rect x="1" y="1" width="410" height="266" rx="8" class="map-felt"/>

            <!-- place numbers -->
            <g class="map-cells">
              <rect x="12" y="12" width="62" height="46" rx="4" class="map-box"/>
              <text x="43" y="34" class="map-num" text-anchor="middle">4</text>
              <text x="43" y="48" class="map-fine" text-anchor="middle">9:5</text>
              <rect x="78" y="12" width="62" height="46" rx="4" class="map-box"/>
              <text x="109" y="34" class="map-num" text-anchor="middle">5</text>
              <text x="109" y="48" class="map-fine" text-anchor="middle">7:5</text>
              <rect x="144" y="12" width="62" height="46" rx="4" class="map-box"/>
              <text x="175" y="34" class="map-num" text-anchor="middle">SIX</text>
              <text x="175" y="48" class="map-fine" text-anchor="middle">7:6</text>
              <rect x="210" y="12" width="62" height="46" rx="4" class="map-box"/>
              <text x="241" y="34" class="map-num" text-anchor="middle">8</text>
              <text x="241" y="48" class="map-fine" text-anchor="middle">7:6</text>
              <rect x="276" y="12" width="62" height="46" rx="4" class="map-box"/>
              <text x="307" y="34" class="map-num" text-anchor="middle">NINE</text>
              <text x="307" y="48" class="map-fine" text-anchor="middle">7:5</text>
              <rect x="342" y="12" width="57" height="46" rx="4" class="map-box"/>
              <text x="370" y="34" class="map-num" text-anchor="middle">10</text>
              <text x="370" y="48" class="map-fine" text-anchor="middle">9:5</text>
            </g>

            <!-- come -->
            <rect x="12" y="66" width="387" height="46" rx="4" class="map-box"/>
            <text x="24" y="86" class="map-label">COME</text>
            <text x="24" y="102" class="map-fine">bet here once a point is on</text>

            <!-- field -->
            <rect x="12" y="120" width="387" height="46" rx="4" class="map-box"/>
            <text x="24" y="140" class="map-label">FIELD</text>
            <text x="24" y="156" class="map-fine">2 3 4 9 10 11 12 &nbsp;&middot;&nbsp; one roll only</text>

            <!-- don't pass -->
            <rect x="12" y="174" width="387" height="38" rx="4" class="map-box-dont"/>
            <text x="24" y="198" class="map-label">DON'T PASS BAR</text>

            <!-- pass line -->
            <rect x="12" y="220" width="387" height="36" rx="4" class="map-box-pass"/>
            <text x="24" y="243" class="map-label-dark">PASS LINE</text>

            <!-- props box -->
            <rect x="421" y="12" width="118" height="154" rx="6" class="map-box-prop"/>
            <text x="480" y="32" class="map-label" text-anchor="middle">HARDWAYS</text>
            <text x="480" y="50" class="map-fine" text-anchor="middle">hard 4 &middot; 10 &nbsp;7:1</text>
            <text x="480" y="64" class="map-fine" text-anchor="middle">hard 6 &middot; 8 &nbsp;9:1</text>
            <path d="M433 78 H 527" class="dgm-rule"/>
            <text x="480" y="98" class="map-label" text-anchor="middle">ONE-ROLL</text>
            <text x="480" y="116" class="map-fine" text-anchor="middle">any craps 7:1</text>
            <text x="480" y="130" class="map-fine" text-anchor="middle">any seven 4:1</text>
            <text x="480" y="152" class="map-fine-warn" text-anchor="middle">worst odds here</text>

            <text x="421" y="192" class="map-fine">Outer band = the two</text>
            <text x="421" y="206" class="map-fine">bets you make first.</text>
            <text x="421" y="228" class="map-fine">Top row = numbers</text>
            <text x="421" y="242" class="map-fine">you can bet anytime.</text>
          </svg>
        </div>
        <figcaption>
          The layout above the fold uses this same grouping — line bets, then numbers,
          then one-roll props.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p>
        The <b>outer band</b> (Pass Line and Don't Pass) is where a round begins. The
        <b>number boxes</b> along the top are bets you can make at any time. The
        <b>centre box</b> — hardways and one-roll props — is the loudest part of a real
        table and the most expensive place to play.
      </p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details class="accordion">
    <summary>5 &middot; The bets, best to worst</summary>
    <div class="accordion-body">
      <p>
        Every bet below exists on the table above. They're ordered by how much the house
        keeps — the top of this list is where a thought-out session lives.
      </p>

      <h3 class="learn-h3">Start here</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <b>Pass Line</b> — the default bet. Even money. Place it on the come-out roll,
          before a point is set.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>Odds</b> — once a point is on, you can back your Pass Line bet with an extra
          wager that pays <i>true odds</i>: <b>{{ odds.trueOddsStr[4] }}</b> on a point of 4
          or 10, <b>{{ odds.trueOddsStr[5] }}</b> on 5 or 9, <b>{{ odds.trueOddsStr[6] }}</b>
          on 6 or 8. This is the only bet on the table with <b>no house edge at all</b>. The
          table above uses the standard 3-4-5x caps, so you can back a point of 6 or 8 with
          up to {{ odds.oddsMult[6] }} times your Pass Line bet.
          Use the <b>+ Odds</b> button in the bet sheet.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>Come / Don't Come</b> — the same bet as Pass/Don't Pass, but made
          <i>after</i> a point is already on. It gets its own personal point and travels to
          that number. A way to have action on more than one number at once.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>Place 6 and Place 8</b> — bet a number directly and win if it repeats before a
          7. Pays <b>{{ odds.placeOddsStr[6] }}</b>, so bet these in multiples of 6. Quietly
          one of the better bets on the layout, and unlike the Pass Line you can make it
          whenever you like.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h3 class="learn-h3">Situational</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <b>Don't Pass</b> — the mirror image: you win when the Pass Line loses. Wins on
          2 or 3 on the come-out, loses on 7 or 11, and <b>pushes on 12</b> (your bet comes
          back). Slightly better odds than the Pass Line, and slightly lonely at a real table.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>Place 5 and 9</b> — pays <b>{{ odds.placeOddsStr[5] }}</b>. Reasonable, not great.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>Buy 4 or 10</b> — pays the true <b>{{ odds.trueOddsStr[4] }}</b> instead of
          Place's {{ odds.placeOddsStr[4] }}, but charges a {{ odds.buyCommissionPct }}%
          commission up front. On 4 and 10 that trade is worth it; on any other number
          it isn't.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h3 class="learn-h3">Paying for excitement</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><b>Field</b> — one roll only. Wins on 2, 3, 4, 9, 10, 11, 12;
          <b>2 pays {{ odds.fieldPayStr[2] }}</b> and <b>12 pays {{ odds.fieldPayStr[12] }}</b>,
          the rest even money. Looks like it covers most of the
          board, but 5, 6, 7 and 8 are the most common totals and none of them are in it.</li>
        <li><b>Hardways</b> — betting a number comes up as a double: hard 6 is
          <span class="pips">&#9858;&#9858;</span>, not
          <span class="pips">&#9857;&#9859;</span>. Hard 4 and 10 pay <b>{{ odds.hardPay[4] }}:1</b>,
          hard 6 and 8 pay <b>{{ odds.hardPay[6] }}:1</b>. Loses if a 7 shows or if the number
          rolls the easy way first.</li>
        <li><b>Any Craps</b> — one roll, wins on 2, 3 or 12, pays <b>{{ odds.anyCrapsPayStr }}</b>.</li>
        <li><b>Any Seven</b> — one roll, wins on 7, pays <b>{{ odds.anySevenPayStr }}</b>. The worst bet available.</li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        For the exact house edge on each of these, see the
        <a href="/blog/craps-house-edge-by-bet/">full ranking</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details class="accordion">
    <summary>6 &middot; A full round, step by step</summary>
    <div class="accordion-body">
      <p>
        Here's one complete round with real numbers, the way it would read in the log on
        the table above. Starting bankroll: <b>$300</b>.
      </p>

      <ol class="learn-steps">
        <li>
          <b>Bet $10 on the Pass Line.</b> Bankroll $290. The status bar reads
          <i>OFF (come-out)</i>.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>Come-out roll: <span class="pips">&#9858;&#9860;</span> = 8.</b>
          Not a 7, 11, or craps — so <b>8 becomes the point</b>. The Pass Line bet stays up
          and now needs an 8 before a 7.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>Back it with $50 in Odds.</b> The point is 8, so the cap is 5&times; your $10
          line bet. Bankroll $240. This part of your bet has zero house edge.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>Roll: <span class="pips">&#9860;&#9856;</span> = 6.</b> Nothing happens — 6 is
          neither the point nor a 7. The shooter keeps the dice.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>Place the 6 for $12.</b> Bankroll $228. Place 6 pays 7:6, which is why $12 is
          a sensible size — it divides cleanly.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>Roll: <span class="pips">&#9858;&#9858;</span> = 6.</b> Your Place 6 wins
          <b>$14</b> (12 &times; 7 &divide; 6). Bankroll $242, and the $12 bet stays up.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>Roll: <span class="pips">&#9859;&#9859;</span> = 8.</b> The point repeats.
          The Pass Line pays even money — <b>$10</b> — and your $50 in Odds pays 6:5 on a
          point of 8, which is <b>$60</b>. Both original bets come back too.
        </li>
      </ol>

      <p class="learn-note">
        Round result: <b>+$84</b> in winnings, and the round ends. The next roll is a fresh
        come-out, the point resets to <i>OFF</i>, and your Place 6 stays on the table for
        the new round unless you take it down.
      </p>
      <p>
        Notice where the money came from: <b>$60 of that $84 was the Odds bet</b> — the one
        with no house edge. That's the whole argument for keeping your flat bet modest and
        putting your money behind the line instead.
      </p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details class="accordion">
    <summary>7 &middot; Where to go next</summary>
    <div class="accordion-body">
      <p>Once the loop makes sense, these go deeper:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <a href="/strategy/pass-line-and-odds/">The Pass Line and why Odds bets matter</a>
          — how stacking Odds pulls your effective house edge from {{ odds.houseEdge.passLine }}%
          down to about {{ odds.houseEdge.passLineFullOdds }}%.
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="/strategy/bankroll-management/">Bankroll management for craps</a>
          — sizing bets and setting limits so a cold streak doesn't end the session.
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="/blog/craps-house-edge-by-bet/">Craps house edge by bet, ranked</a>
          — every bet on the layout with its actual cost, best to worst.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The fastest way to make any of this stick is to scroll up, set a bankroll, and play
        a few dozen rolls betting only the Pass Line with full Odds. It's a boring strategy.
        It's also the best one on the table.
      </p>
    </div>
  </details>
</section>
